### What is the problem?

Given an array of elements, find all elements that appear more than once. The simplest and most efficient general approach uses a `HashSet` to detect duplicates in a single linear pass.

At the National Bank of India, Rajesh receives a nightly batch of processed transaction IDs from a payment partner. Because of retries, the same ID sometimes appears twice. Posting a duplicate would double-charge a customer like Priya, so before posting Rajesh scans the batch for duplicates.

### Why use a HashSet?

A `HashSet` stores only unique elements and offers average O(1) time for `add`, `contains`, and `remove`. The key insight is the return value of `add()`: it returns `true` when the element is new and `false` when it already exists. That single boolean is the elegant core of duplicate detection.

- No need to call `contains` separately before adding.
- One method call both checks and inserts.
- Average constant time per element, so the full scan is O(n).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class DuplicateDetector {
    public static Set<String> findDuplicates(String[] transactionIds) {
        Set<String> seen = new HashSet<>();
        Set<String> duplicates = new HashSet<>();
        for (String id : transactionIds) {
            if (!seen.add(id)) {
                duplicates.add(id);
            }
        }
        return duplicates;
    }
}
```

### How does the algorithm work step by step?

The algorithm walks through the array once. For each element, it calls `seen.add(element)`. If the return value is `false`, the element was already in the set, so it is a duplicate and gets added to the duplicates set.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class DuplicateDemoStepByStep {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] ids = {"TXN-101", "TXN-102", "TXN-101", "TXN-103", "TXN-102", "TXN-101"};
        Set<String> seen = new HashSet<>();
        Set<String> duplicates = new HashSet<>();

        for (String id : ids) {
            boolean isNew = seen.add(id);
            System.out.println("Processing " + id + " -> add returned " + isNew);
            if (!isNew) {
                duplicates.add(id);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Duplicates: " + duplicates);
    }
}
```

```text
Processing TXN-101 -> add returned true
Processing TXN-102 -> add returned true
Processing TXN-101 -> add returned false
Processing TXN-103 -> add returned true
Processing TXN-102 -> add returned false
Processing TXN-101 -> add returned false
Duplicates: [TXN-101, TXN-102]
```

### Why collect duplicates into a Set rather than a List?

If an element appears three or more times, a `List` would record it as a duplicate on every repeat. A `Set` ensures each duplicate value is reported exactly once.

```java
// Wrong: List records TXN-101 twice as a duplicate
List<String> dupList = new ArrayList<>();
// ids = {"TXN-101", "TXN-101", "TXN-101"}
// dupList becomes [TXN-101, TXN-101]

// Correct: Set records TXN-101 once
Set<String> dupSet = new HashSet<>();
// dupSet becomes [TXN-101]
```

### How to count the frequency of each duplicate?

When you need how many times each value appears, switch from a `HashSet` to a `HashMap` frequency counter.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FrequencyCounter {
    public static Map<String, Integer> countFrequencies(String[] transactionIds) {
        Map<String, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (String id : transactionIds) {
            freq.merge(id, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        return freq;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] ids = {"TXN-101", "TXN-102", "TXN-101", "TXN-103", "TXN-102", "TXN-101"};
        Map<String, Integer> freq = countFrequencies(ids);
        freq.forEach((id, count) -> {
            if (count > 1) {
                System.out.println(id + " appeared " + count + " times");
            }
        });
    }
}
```

```text
TXN-101 appeared 3 times
TXN-102 appeared 2 times
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Approach | Time | Space | Preserves Order? |
|---|---|---|---|
| HashSet scan | O(n) | O(n) | Yes |
| Sorting + neighbour compare | O(n log n) | O(1) | No |
| Brute-force nested loops | O(n^2) | O(1) | Yes |
| HashMap frequency count | O(n) | O(n) | Yes |

The HashSet approach is the standard choice: linear time, linear space, and it preserves the original array order.

### What are common mistakes?

A common mistake is using `contains` followed by `add` as two separate calls, which works but is redundant.

```java
// Redundant: two hash lookups per element
if (seen.contains(id)) {
    duplicates.add(id);
} else {
    seen.add(id);
}

// Clean: one hash lookup per element
if (!seen.add(id)) {
    duplicates.add(id);
}
```

Another mistake when dealing with `BigDecimal` amounts in banking is relying on `equals` instead of `compareTo`. Two `BigDecimal` values like `100` and `100.00` are not equal by `equals` but are equal by `compareTo`.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

BigDecimal a = new BigDecimal("100");
BigDecimal b = new BigDecimal("100.00");
System.out.println(a.equals(b));      // false (different scale)
System.out.println(a.compareTo(b));   // 0 (same value)
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **Empty array**: return an empty set immediately.
- **All elements identical**: every element after the first is a duplicate.
- **No duplicates**: the seen set grows to the array size and the duplicates set stays empty.
- **Null elements**: `HashSet` allows one `null`, so a second `null` is correctly detected as a duplicate.

```java
String[] edge1 = {};                          // result: []
String[] edge2 = {"TXN-101", "TXN-101"};      // result: [TXN-101]
String[] edge3 = {"TXN-101", "TXN-102"};      // result: []
String[] edge4 = {null, "TXN-101", null};     // result: [null]
```

### What about the sorting alternative?

If extra space is not available and modifying the input is acceptable, sort the array first and then compare each element with its neighbour.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class SortBasedDuplicateFinder {
    public static List<String> findDuplicates(String[] ids) {
        Arrays.sort(ids);
        List<String> duplicates = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 1; i < ids.length; i++) {
            if (ids[i].equals(ids[i - 1])) {
                if (duplicates.isEmpty() || !duplicates.get(duplicates.size() - 1).equals(ids[i])) {
                    duplicates.add(ids[i]);
                }
            }
        }
        return duplicates;
    }
}
```

This uses O(1) extra space (ignoring the sort's internal stack) but costs O(n log n) time and destroys the original order.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer considers the space-time tradeoff and the data characteristics.

- **HashSet** is the standard answer: simple, fast, preserves order, but uses O(n) extra memory.
- **Sorting** trades time and order for zero extra space.
- **Bloom filter** is an approximate structure that trades a tiny false-positive rate for drastically less memory, useful for very large or streaming datasets.
- **Bit vector** can replace the HashSet when elements are non-negative integers in a known range, using one bit per possible value.
- In concurrent systems, use `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` or `Collections.synchronizedSet()` to make the set thread-safe.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you find the first duplicate in the order of the array? (Return immediately on the first `add` returning false.)
- How would you find all elements that appear exactly twice? (Use a frequency map and filter for count equals two.)
- Can you solve it in O(1) space without sorting? (Only if elements are in range 0 to n-1, using the index-marking trick.)
- How would you handle this for a stream of data? (Use the HashSet approach since it processes elements one at a time.)
